    </p>
  </section>


  <section class="content-section">
    <h2 id="pp-chats">TornManager Chats (Browser Userscript)</h2>
    <p>
      TornManager Chats is an upcoming companion userscript that runs on Torn.com and lets you create and join
      chat rooms with other TornManager users. This section describes what data the chat feature stores and for
      how long.
    </p>

    <h3>Data Stored on the Server</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rooms</strong> &mdash; the room name, when it was created, and who created it.</li>
      <li><strong>Memberships</strong> &mdash; which rooms your Torn ID belongs to.</li>
      <li><strong>Messages</strong> &mdash; the message text (up to 300 characters), your Torn ID and player name, and a timestamp. Joining or leaving a room also posts a small system message.</li>
      <li><strong>Invite tokens</strong> &mdash; the secret token that a room's invite link contains.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Private rooms are end-to-end encrypted.</strong> Message text is encrypted in your browser before it
      is sent, and the encryption key is contained in the room's invite link and stored only on the devices of
      people who have that link. The key is never sent to my server, so the messages I store for private rooms are
      ciphertext that I cannot read. If you lose the key (for example by clearing browser data), rejoin the room
      through its invite link to restore it. I do still store, and can see, which accounts belong to a private room
      and each message's sender and timestamp — encryption protects the message content, not the fact that a
      conversation took place.
    </p>
    <p>
      Public rooms are <em>not</em> end-to-end encrypted (anyone can join them, so there is no shared secret): their
      messages are stored so they can be delivered, are anonymised as described above, and I am technically able to
      read them to investigate abuse. Do not share API keys, passwords, or other secrets in any chat.
    </p>

    <h3>Public Rooms &amp; Anonymity</h3>
    <p>
      Some rooms are public and anonymous: anyone using TornManager can join them and talk under a pseudonym
      (for example "Silent Bandit 42") rather than their Torn name. Each account is assigned one pseudonym that
      stays the same forever and across every public room, so leaving and rejoining does not change it. Messages
      you send in public rooms are stored with your pseudonym and <strong>without your Torn ID</strong>, so other
      members cannot identify you from the chat. As the operator I can still link a pseudonym to an account when
      required to investigate abuse. Anonymity applies to other players, not to me.
    </p>

    <h3>Retention</h3>
    <p>
      Chat data is short-lived by design. A private room &mdash; including all of its messages &mdash; is
      permanently deleted from the database when the last member leaves, and private rooms with no new messages
      for 7 days are automatically deleted by a daily cleanup job. Public rooms are permanent, but their messages
      are automatically deleted after 48 hours.
    </p>

    <h3>Local Storage (Browser)</h3>
    <p>The chats userscript stores the following data in your browser's localStorage on the torn.com domain:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>User profile and API key</strong> (<code>tm_user</code>) &mdash; your Torn ID, name, and API key for authentication. Cleared when you remove your API key from the userscript.</li>
      <li><strong>Error log</strong> (<code>tm_errors</code>) &mdash; up to 50 error entries for debugging, which you can copy or clear from the userscript panel.</li>
      <li><strong>Chat interface state</strong> (<code>tm_chat_open</code>, <code>tm_chat_seen</code>) &mdash; which chat windows are open and the last message you have seen per room, used for unread indicators.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Backend Communication</h3>
    <p>
      The userscript talks exclusively to tornmanager.com using <code>GM.xmlHttpRequest</code>: signing in
      (<code>/api/session</code>) and the chat endpoints (<code>/api/chat/&hellip;</code>) for listing your rooms,
      joining via invite token, sending messages, and fetching new messages (polled every few seconds while a chat
      window is open). Every request includes your API key for authentication. No data is sent to any third party.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2 id="pp-faction-data-sharing">Faction Data Sharing</h2>
    <p>
      TornManager includes features that allow controlled sharing of faction data between factions:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Leadership Access:</strong> When a faction is set up, TornManager fetches faction member data from the Torn API to identify Leaders and Co-leaders. These members are automatically granted access to the Leadership dashboard. The faction leader can modify this whitelist at any time through the Settings section.</li>
      <li><strong>Faction Whitelists:</strong> Faction leaders can configure whitelists to allow members of other factions to view their faction's training compliance data. Only whitelisted faction members can access this data, and the faction leader maintains full control over the whitelist.</li>
      <li><strong>Subscription Sharing:</strong> Faction leaders with active subscriptions can share subscription weeks with their faction members. This is an opt-in feature that helps faction leaders provide TornManager access to their members.</li>
      <li><strong>Member Activity Tracking:</strong> TornManager polls your faction's member status (Online, Idle, or Offline) every 15 minutes using the faction's API key. This data is used to generate activity heatmaps and chain coverage reports visible to faction leadership. Activity snapshots are stored indefinitely and deleted when a faction's data is purged.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Your personal statistics, stock portfolio, and other individual data are never shared with other users or factions.
      Only faction-level aggregate data (training compliance, member activity patterns) can be shared via whitelists.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2 id="pp-spy-reports">Third-Party Data (Spy Reports)</h2>
    <p>
      During live ranked wars, TornManager stores and displays spy reports for enemy faction members. This data includes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Estimated battle stats (strength, speed, defense, dexterity)</li>
      <li>Spy report timestamp and source</li>
      <li>Player status and hospital timers</li>
      <li>Online activity and last seen timestamps</li>
      <li>Travel destinations and abroad locations</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This information is sourced from the TornStats API and the Torn API, and represents publicly available spy data.
      It is used solely to assist faction members during ranked wars and is displayed on the TornManager web
      application. The data is cached temporarily on the server
      (refreshed every 6 seconds during active wars) and is not permanently stored.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are an enemy faction member whose data is displayed, this data is retrieved from public 
      sources and is not obtained through any unauthorized means.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2 id="pp-third-party-services">Third-Party Services</h2>
    <p>TornManager integrates with the following third-party services:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Torn API:</strong> The official Torn.com API is used to fetch your game data. Your information is shared with Torn's servers only when fetching updates via the API.</li>
      <li><strong>TornStats API:</strong> During live ranked wars, TornManager fetches spy reports from the TornStats API to display estimated battle stats for enemy faction members. This helps faction leaders make tactical decisions. The spy data retrieved includes estimated battle stats (strength, speed, defense, dexterity) and is cached temporarily. No data is sent to TornStats - only publicly available spy information is retrieved.</li>
      <li><strong>AppSignal:</strong> Used for application performance monitoring and error tracking. AppSignal receives only anonymized technical metrics (response times, error counts) to help maintain service reliability. No personal data, Torn IDs, or game information is sent to AppSignal.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I do not sell or share your personal data with any other third parties for marketing or advertising purposes.</p>
  </section>

